sudo systemctl status jenkins	                              Check status
sudo systemctl enable jenkins	                              Start automatically at boot


4. Create a simple Jenkins job
In the Jenkins web interface:
1) Click New Item.
2) Enter a job name, e.g. Hello-World.
3) Select Freestyle project.
4) Click OK.
5) Under Build Steps, select Execute shell.
6) Enter:
echo "Hello World"


7) Click Save.
8) Click Build Now.
9) Open the build and select Console Output to see the result.


5. Jenkins Hello World
In Execute shell, use:
echo "Hello World"

Expected output in Console Output:
Hello World

This verifies that Jenkins can execute a basic command.


6. GitHub and Jenkins
Purpose: Jenkins gets source code from a GitHub repository and runs a build.
Commands to check Git
git --version

Checks Git installation.
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

Clones a GitHub repository.

Configure in Jenkins
1) Create a Freestyle project.
2) Under Source Code Management, select Git.
3) Enter the GitHub repository URL.
4) Add credentials if the repository is private.
5) Under Build Steps → Execute shell, enter:
echo "Code pulled from GitHub"

6) Save and click Build Now.
7) Check Console Output.
Remember: GitHub stores the code; Jenkins automates the build.


7. Docker and Jenkins
Purpose: Jenkins runs Docker commands to build or run containers.

Check Docker:
docker --version

Checks Docker installation.
sudo systemctl start docker

Starts Docker.
sudo docker ps

Lists running containers.

Build a Docker image through Jenkins
In Jenkins Execute shell, assuming a Dockerfile is present in the job’s workspace:
docker build -t myapp .

Builds an image named myapp.
docker images

Lists Docker images.
docker run -d -p 8081:80 --name mycontainer myapp

Runs the image as a container and maps host port 8081 to container port 80.


